research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-8-{"id":"lem-metric-uniformity-dictionary","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the metric entourages, induced metric topology, separation, and epsilon-delta interpretation. The item packages these clauses. Unlike the source convention, the library permits an empty metric carrier while requiring filters proper; this creates the empty-carrier defect escalated in A3.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-9-{"id":"def-uniform-cover-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Encyclopedia of Mathematics gives the uniform-cover axioms and Wodzicki develops the associated cover relation. The item uses an equivalent star-refinement presentation and currently overstates equivalence on the empty carrier under the library proper-filter convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-10-{"id":"lem-entourage-and-uniform-cover-dictionary","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the standard equivalence between entourage and uniform-cover formulations. The item spells out both constructions; its cover-to-entourage direction needs the nonempty-carrier qualification recorded for A3.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-11-{"id":"def-gauge-of-pseudometrics","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf","https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources define a uniformity from finite intersections of pseudometric balls. The item calls the family a gauge and bundles topology and uniform-continuity clauses; under the local proper-filter convention it also needs a nonempty carrier.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-12-{"id":"lem-normal-sequences-of-entourages","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Iterated symmetric entourage roots are the standard normal-sequence construction in both sources, but the Statement makes the strictly weaker foundational hypothesis DC explicit and no located source states that same choice-calibrated proposition. It is established, not novel; the recursion proof is local. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Iterating symmetric entourage roots to obtain a decreasing normal sequence with triple-composite control is the classical uniform-space construction. Exposing DC as the recursion principle is a foundational calibration of that standard result, not a novel claim.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-13-{"id":"lem-pseudometric-from-a-normal-entourage-sequence","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"Both sources state the normal-sequence metrization lemma with the same dyadic control inclusions and path-infimum pseudometric construction. The proof is a notation-adjusted expansion.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-14-{"id":"thm-entourage-uniformities-are-generated-by-gauges","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The sources prove every uniformity is generated by pseudometrics but assume an ordinary classical background; the item isolates DC as sufficient. The theorem is standard, but no source with the same foundational hypothesis surfaced. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Representation of an arbitrary uniformity by a family of pseudometrics is a standard uniform-space theorem, and the current proof follows the usual normal-sequence construction. Stating DC as sufficient is an explicit choice calibration, so the local form is established and ai-altered.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-15-{"id":"thm-three-definitions-of-uniform-space-are-equivalent","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~mike/teaching/ss16/general_topology.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Literature gives the entourage-cover equivalence and pseudometric generation, but not this exact compound ZF/DC calibration. This is a standard synthesis, not an invented theorem; the proof composes the preceding results. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: Entourage, uniform-cover, and gauge formulations are the standard equivalent presentations of uniform spaces. The nonempty-carrier repair and split ZF/DC bookkeeping adapt the classical package to this library's proper-filter convention without creating new mathematics.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-16-{"id":"def-cauchy-filter-in-a-uniform-space","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"The sources use the same requirement that for every entourage a filter member have square contained in it; notation and equivalent cover wording are the only differences.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-17-{"id":"lem-convergent-filters-are-cauchy","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki Exercise 41 states exactly that every filter convergent in the uniform topology is Cauchy. The item supplies the standard symmetric-root proof, with a missing direct citation proposed at A3.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl:18:{"id":"lem-cauchy-filter-with-a-cluster-point-converges","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki Exercise 45 states that every adherence point of a Cauchy filter is its convergence point. The proof expands this and needs the induced-topology citation recorded at A3.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-19-{"id":"def-complete-uniform-space","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Complete_uniform_space"],"rationale":"Both sources define completeness as convergence of every Cauchy filter, exactly as the item does.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-20-{"id":"def-uniform-embedding-and-uniform-isomorphism","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"The literature uses the same uniform-isomorphism and subspace-uniformity notions. The item packages the embedding and isomorphism definitions in library notation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-21-{"id":"def-completion-of-a-uniform-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Complete_uniform_space"],"rationale":"The sources define completion by a complete separated target with dense canonical image and induced original uniformity. The item explicitly permits a noninjective Hausdorff reflection map, the equivalent convention for nonseparated inputs.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-22-{"id":"lem-minimal-cauchy-filter-associated-to-a-cauchy-filter","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.boletin.math.org.mx/pdf/2/34/BSMM%282%29.34.11-21.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki 6.1.2-6.1.5 and Hunt give the canonical unique minimal Cauchy filter contained in a Cauchy filter, while Wodzicki 6.1.4-6.1.6 gives the principal-filter instance used by the canonical map. The A4-recovery repair combines those source-backed clauses and supplies the elementary singleton verification, so the materially repaired Statement is ai-altered and semantically sourced.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-23-{"id":"lem-entourages-on-minimal-cauchy-filters-form-a-separated-uniformity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.boletin.math.org.mx/pdf/2/34/BSMM%282%29.34.11-21.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki restricts the standard filter-base uniformity to minimal Cauchy filters and states it is Hausdorff. The item spells out the relation. Its proof omits the nonempty-carrier discharge supplied by the sourced principal-filter clause.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-24-{"id":"lem-minimal-cauchy-filter-space-is-complete","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.boletin.math.org.mx/pdf/2/34/BSMM%282%29.34.11-21.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources construct the completion from minimal Cauchy filters; completeness of that space is part of the construction. The item isolates and expands that component.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-25-{"id":"lem-point-filters-are-dense-in-the-minimal-cauchy-filter-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://www.boletin.math.org.mx/pdf/2/34/BSMM%282%29.34.11-21.pdf"],"rationale":"Wodzicki 6.1.6-6.2.4 gives the point-neighborhood-filter map, uniform continuity, and density. The item packages these conclusions; A3 proposes the member-contains-the-point clause actually used later.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-26-{"id":"thm-hausdorff-completion-of-a-uniform-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Complete_uniform_space"],"rationale":"The sources state existence of a dense complete Hausdorff completion; Wodzicki also identifies when the canonical map reflects the original uniformity. The item packages the reflection and embedding criterion.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-27-{"id":"thm-uniform-completion-universal-property-and-uniqueness","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf","https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space"],"rationale":"Wodzicki 6.2.3 states the same unique extension of every uniformly continuous map into a complete Hausdorff uniform space. Uniqueness of completions is its exact categorical consequence.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-topology-uniform.provenance.jsonl-28-{"id":"def-totally-bounded-uniform-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://encyclopediaofmath.org/wiki/Uniform_space","https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"Encyclopedia of Mathematics defines precompactness by finite uniform-cover refinement, equivalently finitely many entourage balls. The item adopts the standard name totally bounded and the entourage formulation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
